lagrangian-variation-displacement.interpretation:
The equation $\delta L = \sum_{i=1}^{N} 
abla_i L \cdot \delta \mathbf{r}_i =  oldsymbol{\epsilon} \cdot \sum_{i=1}^{N} 
abla_i L = 0$ relates the total variation of the Lagrangian, $\delta L$, to the positions of the particles. Here, $
abla_i L$ is the gradient of the Lagrangian $L$ with respect to the position vector $\mathbf{r}_i$ of the $i$-th particle, representing a generalized force. The term $\delta \mathbf{r}_i$ is an infinitesimal virtual displacement. For a uniform translation, we set $\delta \mathbf{r}_i =  oldsymbol{\epsilon}$ for all $i=1, ..., N$. The equation states that for a system in a homogeneous space, this variation $\delta L$ must be zero. Because the displacement vector $ oldsymbol{\epsilon}$ is arbitrary, this can only be true if the vector sum $\sum_{i=1}^{N} 
abla_i L$ is itself zero. According to the Euler-Lagrange equations, $
abla_i L =  rac{d}{dt}( rac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{\mathbf{r}}_i})$, so the condition becomes $\sum_i \dot{\mathbf{p}}_i =  rac{d}{dt} \sum_i \mathbf{p}_i = 0$, which is the law of conservation of total linear momentum.
